      "Article 50's second limb obliges providers of generative AI to ensure synthetic text, audio, image and video output is marked in a machine-readable format and detectable as AI-generated. The Digital Omnibus split the timeline: systems placed on the market from August 2026 comply immediately; systems already on the market before then have a grace period to 2 December 2026. Deepfake disclosure and the labelling of AI-generated public-interest text (absent human editorial review) sit alongside, as deployer duties from August.",
      "The phrase doing the heavy lifting is machine-readable. A caption reading \"AI-generated\" is a label, not a marking: the requirement contemplates technical detectability — and the June 2026 Code of Practice on transparency describes the expected architecture as layered, combining secured metadata with technical watermarking, supplemented by fingerprinting and logging, because no single technique survives cropping, compression and paraphrase alone. Robustness, interoperability and reliability are the standard, qualified by technical feasibility — a qualification that invites documentation of what was feasible and chosen, not abstention.",
      "Deployers hold the other end: where your organisation publishes synthetic media, deepfake disclosure duties are yours, with carve-outs for evidently artistic or satirical work (where disclosure must not spoil the work) and for assistive editing that doesn't substantially alter meaning. The practical programme is an inventory question first — which systems generate content, which were on the market before August, where does synthetic output leave the building — then a marking architecture aligned to the Code, then verification that the marks survive your actual publication pipeline, because a watermark stripped by the CMS's image compression was a compliance measure only in the meeting where it was described.",
