      "The UK's explanation duty has sharpened. Within the DUAA's safeguards for significant automated decisions sits the obligation to provide the individual with information about the decision — and the ICO's draft guidance presses past the minimal reading: organisations should provide decision-specific information, helping the person understand the particular logic and factors behind their particular outcome. The generic privacy-notice paragraph — \"we use automated processing including profiling\" — describes the weather. The duty asks for the forecast that applied to you.",
      "The bar is functional, not academic. Explanation exists so the other safeguard rights can be exercised: a person cannot make meaningful representations, or contest an outcome, against a decision whose basis is hidden. Which sets the practical standard: main factors, stated plainly, specific to the case, at a level a non-specialist can act on. \"Your application was declined primarily due to income-to-commitment ratio and limited credit history\" is an explanation; \"the model's output fell below threshold\" is a shrug in formal dress.",
      "Capability has to be built upstream. Systems bought without reasoning visibility cannot support decision-specific explanation, however good intentions are downstream — the procurement standard of 4.5 is where this duty is won. Internally, approved explanation formats per decision type, drafted once and populated per case, beat improvisation; the ICO/Turing tradition of distinguishing explanation types — rationale, data, fairness, responsibility — offers a useful skeleton even for organisations that never read the framework.",
