      "Transparency duties are usually framed outward — to users, subjects, regulators. But every outward explanation is drawn from an inward capability: the organisation's own knowledge of what its systems are, do, and touch. Criterion 1.4 built the register as a governance instrument; this criterion completes it as transparency infrastructure — because the same record that anchors classification and oversight is the source document for every disclosure, explanation and declaration the organisation will ever make about its AI.",
      "The transparency-grade register carries, per system: purpose, in plain language; a logic summary a non-specialist can follow; the data categories consumed; the decisions or outputs produced; the owner accountable for accuracy. Plain language is not a stylistic preference — a register written in vendor jargon transfers the opacity problem inward, and an entry the owner cannot explain aloud is an entry that fails its purpose.",
      "The dividends compound. The Art 50 disclosure inventory (4.1) is a register query. The DUAA scoping exercise (5.1) is a register pass. The Provision 29 evidence pack starts from the register's controls view. Buyer due-diligence questionnaires — increasingly the real transparency regime for SMEs — are answered in hours instead of weeks by organisations whose register is current, and answered credibly, because a crisp register signals exactly the operational competence the questionnaire exists to probe.",
      "At maturity the register's currency is telemetry-tracked and the document itself becomes a transaction asset — produced in due diligence as evidence of a governed estate. The failure mode is the split-brain organisation: confident public statements about AI, resting on an internal picture that is partial, stale, or folklore. External transparency built on internal opacity is not transparency. It is optimism with a publication date.",
