      "The final transparency duty points at the organisation's own voice. AI-washing — overstating what your AI does, how autonomous it is, or how rigorously it is governed — has moved from marketing peccadillo to regulatory target, and the exposure is symmetrical: overclaiming capability misleads the market in one direction; overclaiming governance (\"bias-audited,\" \"fully compliant,\" \"human-reviewed\") misleads it in the other, and is the easier of the two to falsify from your own records. In a regime where the FRC expects outcomes rather than boilerplate and the ICO can compel evidence, the gap between public claim and internal reality is not a communications risk. It is a liability with a timestamp.",
      "The control is editorial and unglamorous: public AI claims — marketing, bids, investor materials, annual reports — verified against technical and operational reality before publication, with named sign-off. The verification question is always the same: *if asked to evidence this sentence, what would we produce?* \"Our AI screens applications in seconds\" is checkable. \"Our ethical AI ensures fair outcomes\" commits the organisation to two claims it had better be able to file. The bid corpus deserves particular attention, because bid claims become contractual representations, and the enterprise buyer's diligence team reads them against the questionnaire answers you also supplied.",
      "Wire the claims review into the same machinery as everything else: the register says what systems exist; the testing records (3.3) say what fairness evidence exists; assurance (1.7) says what has been independently verified — a claim that cannot be traced to one of the three does not publish. At maturity, published claims are periodically re-audited as systems change, and corrections are issued when reality moves, because a claim that was true at publication and false in production is only defensible until someone notices the date.",
      "The failure mode is aspiration typeset as fact. The mature position is duller and stronger: say less, evidence all of it — the one marketing strategy that improves under audit.",
