    id: "6.8",
    title: "The declaration: evidencing AI controls the board can sign",
    category: "Security",
    read: "5 min",
    extract: "From the 2026 reporting cycle, boards declare their material controls effective — and the FRC has said IT, cyber and AI are inside the boundary. Signing on assertion is no longer available.",
    body: [
      "Provision 29 of the UK Corporate Governance Code 2024 changed the character of board assurance: an annual declaration on the effectiveness of material internal controls, with the FRC explicit that the boundary encompasses information technology risks, cybersecurity, and new technologies including AI. For boards, the shift is from monitoring to attesting — and attestation is only as strong as the evidence beneath it. A declaration signed on organisational self-belief is a governance document with a discoverable gap at its centre.",
      "This criterion is where the security domain converges into a board artefact. The build has three movements. Scope: which AI systems and controls sit inside the material-controls boundary — a register pass (1.4) filtered for materiality: systems whose failure would compromise reporting integrity, operations, or compliance. The FRC's direction of travel on AI-mediated reporting makes one inclusion near-automatic: where AI touches the production of the numbers and narratives the board publishes, it is a material control area on its face. Evidence: for each in-scope control, the artefacts that demonstrate effectiveness — the threat models (6.1), verified standards (6.2), access logs (6.3), containment drills (6.4), exercised response plans (6.5), tested fallbacks (6.6), supply-chain assessments (6.7) — assembled as a standing evidence pack, not an annual archaeology. Reporting: the FRC's stated preference for outcomes and activities over boilerplate, which this framework's telemetry layer answers directly: control effectiveness described in measured results, because \"we rehearsed containment twice and halved time-to-contain\" is a disclosure, while \"the board takes cyber security seriously\" is a font choice.",
      "The declaration also disciplines everything upstream: controls that cannot produce effectiveness evidence are findings, surfaced by the declaration process a year before a regulator would have surfaced them — the compliance exercise functioning, at maturity, as the audit you give yourself.",
      "The failure mode is the assertion-shaped declaration: signed in good faith, evidenced nowhere, defensible until examined. The mature position inverts it: telemetry-fed effectiveness data flowing to the board on cadence, so the annual declaration is not an event at all — merely the year's last reading of instruments that never stopped running.",
    ],
