    id: "7.8",
    title: "Keeping the humans sharp: skill sustainability under automation",
    category: "Workforce",
    read: "5 min",
    extract: "The fallback plan assumes people who can still do the work. Automation quietly retires that assumption — unless retention is designed, the capability your controls depend on decays on schedule.",
    body: [
      "The last workforce criterion guards against the slowest failure in this framework: capability decay. Automate a task and the human skill behind it begins to atrophy — not through any decision, but through disuse, attrition, and the arrival of staff who never performed the manual version at all. The losses compound invisibly until the day they are needed: the continuity fallback (6.6) staffed by people who no longer remember the process; the meaningful reviewer (3.5, 7.2) whose competence to judge the system's output eroded as the system did more of the judging; the organisation that can no longer evaluate what it can no longer do. Oversight, resilience, and quality all rest on retained human capability — which makes its retention a control, not a nostalgia.",
      "The discipline is a retained-capability decision per consequential automation, taken at adoption (inside the impact assessment, 7.4): what must humans here still be able to do — to oversee this system, to operate without it, to know when it is wrong — and by what mechanism does that capability stay live? The mechanisms are known and inexpensive relative to what they protect: rotation through manual handling of sampled cases; deliberate practice built into the review role (the high-confidence spot-checks of 7.3 doing double duty); verification duties that keep judgement exercised rather than delegated; and succession attention to the skills that currently live in two heads near retirement.",
      "Wellbeing belongs in the same review, because hollowed work cuts both ways: roles reduced to supervising a machine's throughput lose the engagement that made the judgement good, and disengaged oversight is the soft form of no oversight. Monitoring makes the slow failure visible on purpose — capability sampled, wellbeing surveyed, both trending on the people dashboard rather than discovered in the incident review.",
      "The failure mode is the silent assumption: every plan presuming skills that no process any longer maintains. At maturity the trajectory inverts — deliberate design making the organisation more capable as AI use deepens, not less: the machine extending the humans, and the humans, kept sharp, remaining the reason the machine can be trusted at all.",
    ],
